# NewAgentUniverseByDeepSeek — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored; THIS file is committed on purpose, because a
# template with no secrets in it is documentation rather than a credential.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# ============================================================================
# HOW THIS FILE WAS BUILT, AND WHY THE FIRST VERSION OF IT WAS WRONG
# ============================================================================
#
# The first version listed 9 variables and was produced by searching the source for
# `env::var("<LITERAL>")`. That search has a blind spot: this codebase declares several variables as
# constants first —
#
#     pub const TOKENS_ENV: &str = "NAU_API_TOKENS";
#     ...  std::env::var(TOKENS_ENV)  ...
#
# — and a regex for a string literal cannot see them. The result was a template that omitted
# **`NAU_API_TOKENS`**, which is the variable that decides who may write to the node, and
# **`NAU_SANDBOX_BACKEND`**, which decides whether the sandbox runs anything at all.
#
# This version was produced by scanning for BOTH forms (literal and `const *_ENV: &str = "..."`)
# plus `process.env.*` in the JS, and it lists all 23. The method is written down here so the next
# person can re-run it and check, rather than trust:
#
#     # Rust: literals
#     rg 'env::var\("([A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*)"\)' crates --only-matching
#     # Rust: constants
#     rg 'const\s+[A-Z_]+_ENV\s*:\s*&str\s*=\s*"([A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*)"' crates --only-matching
#     # JavaScript
#     rg 'process\.env\.([A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*)' scripts sdks client --only-matching
#
# A deployment sets the RUNTIME block. It does NOT set the BUILD/CI block, and it must not set the
# TEST block — each is separate below and labelled.
#
# ============================================================================
# 1. RUNTIME — SECURITY. READ THIS BLOCK FIRST.
# ============================================================================
#
# The API's default is **deny-all**: with no tokens configured, NO credential authenticates and
# every mutating route is refused. There is no "allow because nothing is configured" branch
# (`crates/nau-node/src/auth.rs`, `Authenticator::deny_all`). That is the right default, and it has
# a consequence a deployment must know:
#
#   * * * A NODE WITH NO `NAU_API_TOKENS` IS READ-ONLY. * * *
#
# It starts, it answers `GET /health`, and every `POST` is refused. This is correct behaviour and it
# looks like a broken API if you have not read this paragraph.

# The token table. Entries separated by `;`, each `id:token[:did][:scope,scope]`.
#
#   NAU_API_TOKENS=alice:s3cret:did:nau:0011223344556677:read,write;ops:0psT0ken:read,write,admin
#
# * `id`     — a stable caller identity. Required.
# * `token`  — the bearer credential. Required.
# * `did`    — optional. When present, a mutating request whose body names a DIFFERENT actor is
#              refused (`api::require_actor`). A credential WITHOUT a did is a service credential
#              and is not identity-bound — a deliberate, documented privilege.
# * scopes   — `read`, `write`, `admin`. A principal carries write authority only if a token was
#              configured for it; there is no public constructor that grants it.
#
# **A malformed entry fails the WHOLE parse** rather than being skipped — the node refuses to start
# rather than starting with a table that silently lost an entry. Check the logs if startup fails.
#
# Generate a token, do not invent one:
#     head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
NAU_API_TOKENS=

# The escape hatch. `1` allows anonymous writes.
#
# It takes effect ONLY when the node is also bound to loopback; a non-loopback bind ignores it.
# Leave it empty in any deployment that anyone else can reach. It exists for a developer running a
# throwaway node on their own machine, and it is a separate variable from the token table so that
# enabling it is a visible act rather than a default.
NAU_API_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS_WRITES=

# ============================================================================
# 2. RUNTIME — SANDBOX. THE SECOND THING A DEPLOYMENT MUST DECIDE.
# ============================================================================

# Selects the execution backend for the sandbox.
#
#   none      (DEFAULT) — `NullExecutor`: the sandbox runs NOTHING. Every execution request is
#                         refused. This is the safe default and it means a node deployed without
#                         this variable cannot run agent code at all.
#   process             — the platform process backend: real execution, isolated by the platform's
#                         process mechanisms.
#
# * * * ANY OTHER VALUE REFUSES TO OPEN A MANAGER. * * *
#
# It does not fall back to a default, and that is deliberate: "a typo in a security setting must not
# select a weaker one" (`crates/nau-node/src/api/sandbox_routes.rs`). So `NAU_SANDBOX_BACKEND=nonee`
# is a startup failure, not a silent downgrade — check the log rather than assuming.
#
# Kernel-level enforcement (AppArmor, eBPF) is a separate question from this variable: it needs host
# privileges and a Linux kernel, and where it is unavailable the code reports a typed refusal rather
# than degrading quietly. Inside a default container it is unavailable.
NAU_SANDBOX_BACKEND=none

# ============================================================================
# 3. RUNTIME — HTTP POLICY
# ============================================================================

# CORS origins, comma-separated. Applied as `allow_origin` per entry
# (`crates/nau-node/src/auth.rs`).
#
# There is no wildcard and a hostile `Origin` is never echoed back. A request carrying an `Origin`
# is refused unless that origin is explicitly listed; an allowed origin is echoed EXACTLY, one
# value, with `Vary: Origin`. Unset means the built-in policy.
#   NAU_API_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com,https://admin.example.com
NAU_API_ORIGINS=

# Host allowlist, comma-separated. Applied as `allow_host` per entry.
#
# * * * THIS IS THE ONE THAT BITES A NON-LOOPBACK DEPLOYMENT * * *
#
# Binds to 127.0.0.1 are unaffected. The moment `--api-addr 0.0.0.0:4002` is used, a request's Host
# header is no longer `127.0.0.1` and the built-in policy refuses it — so the node accepts the
# connection and refuses the request, which reads as a networking fault rather than a policy one.
#
# The check exists for a specific attack and the reason is worth knowing: **DNS rebinding reaches a
# loopback daemon through a hostname the browser resolves to 127.0.0.1**, so validating `Origin`
# alone is not enough. Include every name this node will be reached by, with and without the port.
#   NAU_API_HOSTS=nau.internal,nau.internal:4002,10.0.0.7:4002
NAU_API_HOSTS=

# Log filter, in `tracing`/`env_logger` syntax.
#   RUST_LOG=nau_node=debug,nau_plugin=debug,warn
RUST_LOG=info

# ============================================================================
# 4. RUNTIME — MCP (only when the MCP plugin is enabled)
# ============================================================================
#
# The MCP-over-HTTP surface authenticates separately from the REST API, and it has the same
# deny-all default (`crates/nau-mcp/src/auth.rs`).

# A single shared token. Simpler, and it carries NO per-caller identity — every holder is the same
# caller as far as the node can tell. Use the table below when you need to tell callers apart.
NAU_MCP_TOKEN=

# The token table, same `id:token[:did][:scope,scope]` format as `NAU_API_TOKENS`.
NAU_MCP_TOKENS=

# ============================================================================
# 5. NOT ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES — DO NOT ADD THESE
# ============================================================================
#
# These are command-line flags. Listing them as environment variables would be inventing variables
# that nothing reads, which is the defect this file's own history records. They are collected here
# so that a reader looking for one finds the answer instead of an empty search.
#
#   --api-addr <ADDR>          bind address (default 127.0.0.1:4002)
#   --api-port <PORT>          shorthand for --api-addr 127.0.0.1:<PORT>
#   --data-dir <DIR>           state directory (default nau-data; leading ~ is expanded)
#   --ephemeral                keep everything in memory, persist nothing
#   --min-stake <AMOUNT>       minimum stake to register an agent (default 100)
#   --min-reputation-bps <N>   reputation floor to bid, 0..=10000 (default 0)
#   --version / -h / --help
#
#   # NAU_API_ADDR=...    <-- NOT read by this repository. Use --api-addr.
#   # NAU_DATA_DIR=...    <-- NOT read by this repository. Use --data-dir.

# ============================================================================
# 6. BUILD / CI ONLY — a deployment does NOT set these
# ============================================================================
#
# They exist so `verify-all.mjs` can find tools it does not bundle, and so a constrained machine can
# avoid an out-of-memory build. A production node needs none of them.

# Parallel cargo jobs.
#
# * * * READ THIS BEFORE REMOVING IT * * *
#
# A parallel build of this 18-crate workspace can exhaust the memory of a small machine, and it does
# so SILENTLY: cargo is killed with signal 9, the shell reports a failure with no compiler error, and
# the cause looks like a code problem. `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` records it as a real incident. 2 is safe
# on a 2 GB machine.
CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2

# `forge`, for the contract gates and the contract tests.
FORGE_BIN=

# Python interpreter, for the Python SDK gate.
NAU_PYTHON=

# Rust toolchain selector, used by the gates to pin 1.85.0 rather than whatever the default is.
NAU_RUST=

# Selects the cargo profile `deploy-local.mjs` builds and installs with
# (`scripts/deploy-local.mjs`: `process.env.NAU_PROFILE || (a detected default)`).
#
# It is a variable of THIS REPOSITORY'S SCRIPTS rather than of the node, which is why it lives in
# this block. Setting it changes what the deployment checks exercise — a `release` deployment check
# run is a different test from a `debug` one, and the difference is worth being explicit about.
#   NAU_PROFILE=release
NAU_PROFILE=

# Python interpreter, as `verify-all.mjs` resolves it (`scripts/verify-all.mjs`: `process.env.PYTHON`
# is consulted before the bundled one). Distinct from `NAU_PYTHON` above, which the deploy script
# uses; both exist in the repository and either may be what a given gate reads.
PYTHON=

# Solidity compiler, for the contract build gate.
SOLC_BIN=

# Publish-only. A deployment does not need either; the release job does.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
GH_TOKEN=

# ============================================================================
# 7. TEST-ONLY — set by the test suite itself, never by a deployment
# ============================================================================
#
# Documented so that a reader who greps the source and finds them knows what they are. Setting any
# of these in a deployment changes test behaviour, not node behaviour.

# Enables fixture writing for the plugin tests.
NAU_PLUGIN_WRITE_FIXTURES=

# Drives the Windows sandbox resume-probe test.
NAU_RESUME_PROBE=

# Reads a payload from stdin in a sandbox test.
NAU_SANDBOX_TEST_READ_STDIN=

# Sleeps for N milliseconds in a sandbox test.
NAU_SANDBOX_TEST_SLEEP_MS=

# A test payload signing key for the provider-payment tests.
NAU_TEST_PROVIDER_PAYLOAD_KEY=
